+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole number of seconds, like
+`120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h`
+(hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds), like `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`.
+Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, with no spaces. Units are
+case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Negative and fractional
+values are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
